1
00:00:00,501 --> 00:00:02,549
Everybody plays Uno a
little bit differently.

2
00:00:03,140 --> 00:00:04,942
Some people will allow plus twos to stack.

3
00:00:05,502 --> 00:00:08,525
Some people will say that you have to swap
hands with someone whenever you play a

4
00:00:08,605 --> 00:00:11,887
seven. And some people will force you to
keep drawing and drawing until you can

5
00:00:11,927 --> 00:00:14,930
play, and somebody else will always
say that that isn't a real rule.

6
00:00:15,460 --> 00:00:18,165
So, I took every house rule I
could find, gave them to bots,

7
00:00:18,446 --> 00:00:20,369
and played over twenty
three billion rounds of Uno.

8
00:00:21,442 --> 00:00:22,786
I wanted to find the best way to play.

9
00:00:23,228 --> 00:00:26,278
Rounds that don't drag on forever, and
where playing well actually wins you more

10
00:00:26,298 --> 00:00:27,098
games.

11
00:00:27,170 --> 00:00:29,232
The rules decide how
much skill Uno even has.

12
00:00:29,633 --> 00:00:32,255
Some rule sets let a good player
win more than a third of the time.

13
00:00:32,776 --> 00:00:35,999
One makes playing well lose you games,
and a bunch of the rules you think are

14
00:00:36,019 --> 00:00:37,160
official actually aren't.

15
00:00:40,481 --> 00:00:42,984
If it's been a while since you've
played, the rule sheet goes like this.

16
00:00:43,484 --> 00:00:47,129
Everyone gets seven cards, and one card
gets flipped face up from the deck to start

17
00:00:47,149 --> 00:00:47,949
the pile.

18
00:00:48,020 --> 00:00:51,404
On your turn, you can play a card that
matches the top card either by color or by

19
00:00:51,464 --> 00:00:53,127
number, or you can play a Wild.

20
00:00:53,547 --> 00:00:55,570
The first one to get to
zero cards wins the game.

21
00:00:56,410 --> 00:00:58,074
A Skip makes the next player miss a turn.

22
00:00:58,535 --> 00:00:59,718
Reverse flips the direction.

23
00:01:00,159 --> 00:01:02,725
Draw Two makes the next player
draw two and they lose their turn.

24
00:01:03,166 --> 00:01:04,369
A Wild lets you pick the color.

25
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:06,941
Now, the Wild Draw Four.

26
00:01:07,441 --> 00:01:09,383
I bet that most people have
never even heard of this part.

27
00:01:09,803 --> 00:01:12,506
Officially, you can only play it when you
have nothing left in the current color.

28
00:01:12,966 --> 00:01:14,868
If someone thinks you cheated,
they can challenge you,

29
00:01:15,168 --> 00:01:17,450
and if you get caught, you have
to draw the four cards yourself.

30
00:01:18,230 --> 00:01:20,477
And fun fact, that challenge
barely even matters anyway.

31
00:01:20,858 --> 00:01:24,249
Challenging every plus four or bluffing
one changes your win rate by about half a

32
00:01:24,289 --> 00:01:25,089
point.

33
00:01:25,262 --> 00:01:27,307
The sheet also says that
you can't stack plus twos.

34
00:01:27,708 --> 00:01:29,472
And when you can't
play, you draw one card.

35
00:01:29,913 --> 00:01:32,659
One. Not to draw cards until
you find something you can play.

36
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:35,264
I checked my simulator against plain math.

37
00:01:35,605 --> 00:01:38,812
The chance that your opening hand has at
least one Wild is fifty five point eight

38
00:01:38,853 --> 00:01:41,919
percent, and over ten million
deals my simulator got the same.

39
00:01:42,750 --> 00:01:44,855
Also, the bots only know what
a real player would know.

40
00:01:45,155 --> 00:01:48,021
Their own hand, the top card, and
how many cards everyone's holding.

41
00:01:48,502 --> 00:01:51,870
A normal bot plays any card that matches
and picks the color it holds the most of.

42
00:01:53,330 --> 00:01:55,013
How do you even measure
the skill in a card game?

43
00:01:55,333 --> 00:01:58,317
For me, I took my best bot, gave
it every good habit I could find,

44
00:01:58,598 --> 00:02:00,180
and sat it down with three normal players.

45
00:02:00,710 --> 00:02:02,896
If Uno was pure luck, it
would win one round in four,

46
00:02:03,196 --> 00:02:04,420
exactly twenty five percent.

47
00:02:05,240 --> 00:02:08,144
Under the official rules, it wins
twenty nine, four points of skill.

48
00:02:08,605 --> 00:02:11,448
If we follow the official rules, most of
the game is just the cards that you're

49
00:02:11,468 --> 00:02:12,490
dealt and the cards you draw.

50
00:02:12,991 --> 00:02:15,135
And for comparison, when I
tested Exploding Kittens,

51
00:02:15,295 --> 00:02:18,843
my best bot won forty and a half percent
of the time at a table of the same size.

52
00:02:19,524 --> 00:02:22,149
So the official version of Uno
sits pretty close to the luck end.

53
00:02:22,961 --> 00:02:25,199
Now let's check each of the
house rules, one at a time.

54
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:28,844
First is stacking, which I guess is the
rule that everybody has looked up at least

55
00:02:28,965 --> 00:02:31,588
once. This rule nudges the
best bot to thirty percent,

56
00:02:31,928 --> 00:02:32,830
that's barely anything.

57
00:02:33,670 --> 00:02:37,494
Next is Seven Zero, where playing a seven
swaps your hand with someone and playing a

58
00:02:37,554 --> 00:02:39,495
zero passes everyone's
hand around the table.

59
00:02:40,036 --> 00:02:42,158
This drags down the win
rate to twenty six percent.

60
00:02:42,678 --> 00:02:46,061
My guess is that it's hard to plan a hand
strategically when someone can just take

61
00:02:46,081 --> 00:02:47,062
it from you at any time.

62
00:02:47,543 --> 00:02:49,544
This rule also wipes out
the edge of going first.

63
00:02:49,965 --> 00:02:53,508
If we follow the official rules, whoever
starts wins almost twenty seven percent of

64
00:02:53,548 --> 00:02:55,590
rounds, with Seven Zero it's dead even.

65
00:02:56,450 --> 00:02:59,975
Now we can look at Jump In, where anyone
holding the exact same card can slap it

66
00:03:00,015 --> 00:03:03,340
down out of turn, and this pushes
it up to thirty and a half percent.

67
00:03:04,160 --> 00:03:08,003
Now the free plus four is how most people
play it, a plus four at any time you want

68
00:03:08,063 --> 00:03:10,825
with no challenges. This brings
us up to thirty one percent.

69
00:03:11,245 --> 00:03:14,528
That's the highest of any single house
rule, and I've essentially played it like

70
00:03:14,528 --> 00:03:17,090
this my whole life without even
knowing it wasn't a real rule.

71
00:03:17,870 --> 00:03:20,753
The rule that says that you have to draw
until you can play barely moves the meter

72
00:03:20,833 --> 00:03:23,275
at all. All it does is make
everything take forever.

73
00:03:23,815 --> 00:03:26,097
Rounds take eighty two turns
instead of forty seven normally,

74
00:03:26,437 --> 00:03:29,380
and one round in ten even takes
over a hundred and seventy turns.

75
00:03:30,213 --> 00:03:33,819
The rule that actually kills skill is not
being allowed to go out on an action card

76
00:03:33,899 --> 00:03:37,005
or a Wild. This drops the meter
to twenty five and a half percent,

77
00:03:37,265 --> 00:03:39,729
which is basically pure luck,
and the rounds get longer too.

78
00:03:40,561 --> 00:03:42,764
And what if you throw every
house rule in at once?

79
00:03:43,185 --> 00:03:46,029
Stacking, Seven Zero, Jump
In, draw until you can play,

80
00:03:46,450 --> 00:03:49,114
the free plus four. You get
eighty six turns a round,

81
00:03:49,474 --> 00:03:52,339
and less skill than the official game
at twenty eight and a half percent.

82
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:56,884
Another thing I tried for fun was Bounce,
where a Reverse sends a plus two right

83
00:03:56,904 --> 00:03:57,885
back at whoever played it.

84
00:03:58,366 --> 00:04:00,768
The win rate stays at twenty
nine, it changes nothing,

85
00:04:01,089 --> 00:04:02,030
it's just really fun to do.

86
00:04:02,910 --> 00:04:04,031
This is how I've always played.

87
00:04:04,331 --> 00:04:06,714
Plus twos stack on plus twos,
and plus fours on plus fours,

88
00:04:06,714 --> 00:04:07,514
but you can't mix them.

89
00:04:07,935 --> 00:04:11,198
You can play a plus four whenever you
want, and if you have a bunch of the same

90
00:04:11,218 --> 00:04:12,660
number you can dump them all at once.

91
00:04:13,420 --> 00:04:14,702
This last one changes a lot.

92
00:04:15,063 --> 00:04:17,226
Rounds drop from forty
seven turns to around forty,

93
00:04:17,527 --> 00:04:19,570
and my best bot wins thirty
one percent of the time.

94
00:04:20,081 --> 00:04:22,706
Out of the one hundred and ninety
two rule combinations I tested,

95
00:04:22,927 --> 00:04:25,091
this combination lands in
the top sixth for skill.

96
00:04:25,652 --> 00:04:28,919
So apparently I've just been playing one
of the better versions of Uno my entire

97
00:04:28,959 --> 00:04:29,759
life.

98
00:04:30,010 --> 00:04:31,792
One habit matters a ton
with this combination.

99
00:04:32,192 --> 00:04:34,515
If you hold back and play your
matching numbers one at a time,

100
00:04:34,775 --> 00:04:36,837
you win fourteen percent of
rounds instead of twenty five.

101
00:04:37,277 --> 00:04:39,520
Basically, if you can dump
your numbers, just dump them.

102
00:04:40,413 --> 00:04:41,836
I also checked how big the stacks get.

103
00:04:42,136 --> 00:04:45,803
With my rules, in about one round of nine,
somebody has to pick up eight or more

104
00:04:45,823 --> 00:04:49,130
cards at once. Ten or more happens
in less than one round in a hundred.

105
00:04:49,862 --> 00:04:51,844
If you mostly play one
on one, the meter shifts.

106
00:04:52,204 --> 00:04:54,927
My best bot wins fifty four percent
against a fair share of fifty,

107
00:04:55,347 --> 00:04:57,990
and whoever goes first wins fifty
one and a half percent of the time.

108
00:04:58,490 --> 00:05:01,335
With Seven Zero, every seven swaps
your hand with your only opponent,

109
00:05:01,655 --> 00:05:04,019
and the best bot falls to
barely over fifty percent.

110
00:05:04,810 --> 00:05:08,481
Also, if we have more people, every
extra player adds around seven turns,

111
00:05:08,842 --> 00:05:11,209
so a ten player round takes
around ninety one turns.

112
00:05:12,022 --> 00:05:13,867
What if you wanted Uno
to just be pure luck?

113
00:05:14,188 --> 00:05:17,958
That's really easy. After every single
turn, just force everyone to pass their hand

114
00:05:18,018 --> 00:05:18,818
to the next player.

115
00:05:19,482 --> 00:05:22,748
My best bot drops to twenty four percent,
which is below a fair share of twenty

116
00:05:22,828 --> 00:05:25,913
five. Under that rule, being good
at Uno makes you worse at Uno.

117
00:05:26,474 --> 00:05:29,400
My guess is that every good move you
set up gets handed to the next person.

118
00:05:30,252 --> 00:05:33,137
Or you could just use one of the ideas
printed on the official rule sheet for the

119
00:05:33,158 --> 00:05:35,522
blank cards. You tell a
joke, and if anyone laughs,

120
00:05:35,643 --> 00:05:37,106
you get to discard down to one card.

121
00:05:37,506 --> 00:05:39,029
If nobody laughs, you have to draw four.

122
00:05:39,940 --> 00:05:42,883
I simulated a laugh as a coin flip,
because my bots aren't funny enough.

123
00:05:43,304 --> 00:05:45,766
This rule drags the meter down
to twenty six and a half percent.

124
00:05:46,167 --> 00:05:48,830
The official suggestion turns
Uno into mostly a coin toss.

125
00:05:49,660 --> 00:05:52,682
Without any silly rules, the most
luck-driven Uno I found is this one.

126
00:05:53,043 --> 00:05:55,244
Draw until you can play,
dump your matching numbers,

127
00:05:55,525 --> 00:05:56,966
and no going out on an action card.

128
00:05:57,406 --> 00:06:00,128
The best bot wins twenty three and a
half, which is below the fair share.

129
00:06:00,529 --> 00:06:02,210
In my opinion, nobody
should play like this.

130
00:06:02,921 --> 00:06:05,203
On the other hand, here's the
most skillful Uno I could find.

131
00:06:05,624 --> 00:06:08,407
Stacking, Jump In, the free
plus four, and, of all things,

132
00:06:08,487 --> 00:06:09,288
draw until you can play.

133
00:06:09,728 --> 00:06:11,310
The best bot wins thirty five percent.

134
00:06:11,681 --> 00:06:14,013
The only catch is that a round
now takes eighty four turns,

135
00:06:14,193 --> 00:06:15,500
almost double the official version.

136
00:06:16,235 --> 00:06:19,809
Now here's what I would play if you want
games to be both fast and skill based.

137
00:06:20,330 --> 00:06:23,573
Stacking, Jump In, the free plus four,
and dumping all your matching numbers at

138
00:06:23,673 --> 00:06:27,736
once. Rounds now take around thirty nine
turns, which is faster than the official

139
00:06:27,776 --> 00:06:29,898
game, and the best bot
wins thirty two percent.

140
00:06:30,198 --> 00:06:32,240
That's almost twice the
skill of the official rules.

141
00:06:32,751 --> 00:06:35,437
If you'd rather keep it a coin toss
so anyone at the table can win,

142
00:06:35,577 --> 00:06:36,499
just add Seven Zero.

143
00:06:37,390 --> 00:06:38,854
Now how do you win under those rules?

144
00:06:39,155 --> 00:06:40,539
There are four big habits to follow.

145
00:06:41,021 --> 00:06:44,149
Every number is one bot changing one
habit against three normal players.

146
00:06:44,951 --> 00:06:47,434
Number one. Dump every
card of a number together.

147
00:06:47,795 --> 00:06:50,840
If you hold any back, you drop from winning
twenty five percent to fifteen percent.

148
00:06:51,651 --> 00:06:52,993
Number two. Hold your Wilds.

149
00:06:53,254 --> 00:06:55,517
Don't play a Wild while you
have another card you can play.

150
00:06:55,817 --> 00:06:57,640
That alone gets you to
twenty nine percent.

151
00:06:58,440 --> 00:07:00,022
Number three. Always stack when you can.

152
00:07:00,383 --> 00:07:03,287
A bot that never stacks, and just takes
the cards instead of passing them on,

153
00:07:03,647 --> 00:07:05,510
wins around twenty two and
a half percent of the time.

154
00:07:06,291 --> 00:07:07,554
The rest is all just small stuff.

155
00:07:08,056 --> 00:07:10,823
Switching into the color you hold most
of is worth about a point and a half,

156
00:07:11,284 --> 00:07:13,189
and jumping in whenever
you can is worth about one.

157
00:07:13,690 --> 00:07:15,815
Finally there's one habit
that beats almost all of them,

158
00:07:16,175 --> 00:07:18,280
and it's the one that my bots
were never allowed to get wrong.

159
00:07:19,070 --> 00:07:21,077
Remember how my bots never
argue about the rules?

160
00:07:21,419 --> 00:07:22,944
They also never forget to say Uno.

161
00:07:23,426 --> 00:07:24,449
So I made them forget.

162
00:07:24,950 --> 00:07:27,997
Officially, if you forget and someone
catches you before the next player goes,

163
00:07:28,098 --> 00:07:28,919
you draw two cards.

164
00:07:29,460 --> 00:07:32,483
I set the table so everyone forgets
to say Uno one time in four,

165
00:07:32,923 --> 00:07:34,785
and someone catches a
forgetter half the time.

166
00:07:35,346 --> 00:07:38,709
A bot that never forgets wins thirty
percent, which is a bigger edge than holding

167
00:07:38,769 --> 00:07:39,569
onto your Wilds.

168
00:07:40,333 --> 00:07:42,599
A bot that always forgets
wins five percent of the time.

169
00:07:42,939 --> 00:07:45,666
Catching other people that forget
barely matters, less than a point.

170
00:07:45,986 --> 00:07:47,229
So just make sure you say Uno.

171
00:07:47,821 --> 00:07:50,506
Also, some tables make you draw
four cards instead of just two.

172
00:07:51,027 --> 00:07:53,251
Then never forgetting gets
you to thirty two percent.

173
00:07:53,712 --> 00:07:55,996
Under the rules I would play,
it lands at twenty nine,

174
00:07:55,996 --> 00:07:57,639
right up there with
holding onto your Wilds.

175
00:07:58,431 --> 00:07:59,832
And at a lot of tables, it's a race.

176
00:08:00,212 --> 00:08:03,536
The second you're down to one card, if
someone else yells Uno before you do,

177
00:08:03,736 --> 00:08:05,918
you draw two. I made
that race a coin flip.

178
00:08:06,359 --> 00:08:08,981
A bot that always wins the race
wins forty one percent of rounds.

179
00:08:09,462 --> 00:08:13,025
A bot that always loses, under the
official rules, basically never wins.

180
00:08:13,626 --> 00:08:14,767
Three rounds in a hundred thousand.

181
00:08:14,987 --> 00:08:17,270
Every time it gets to one
card, it gets back up to three.

182
00:08:18,150 --> 00:08:20,272
Under the official rules,
Uno is mostly luck.

183
00:08:20,692 --> 00:08:23,975
Under the rules I would play, a good
player gets almost twice the edge,

184
00:08:24,275 --> 00:08:26,978
and a round is over in thirty nine
turns, about eight turns faster.

185
00:08:27,418 --> 00:08:29,260
That's the best way to
play Uno that I found.

186
00:08:29,790 --> 00:08:33,488
Next up is Sorry. It's supposed to be pure
luck, but my best bot won a third of its

187
00:08:33,508 --> 00:08:34,304
games.

188
00:08:34,304 --> 00:08:37,195
Tell me where your table lands on the
meter, and what rule you'd add to it.

189
00:08:37,616 --> 00:08:38,319
I'll read every one.
